David Damerell wrote:
> Quoting  Janis Papanagnou  <janis_papanagnou@hotmail.com>:
>> Besides the previously noted aspect there's other sorts of balance; e.g.
>> whether the various monsters are managable with the options available to
>> a player, whether the powerful objects have appropriate drawbacks, etc.
>> To keep this type of balance is more difficult if games have a lot of
>> entities (objects, monsters, dungeons) and a high degree of randomness
>> in their generation. Given Nethack's complexity it does keep that type
>> of balance very well.
> 
> It does? What's the drawback to a GDSM or SDSM? Not only does it grant a
> great resistance, it also grants great AC and is relatively light! What's
> the drawback to Grayswandir? Excalibur? The powerful objects tend to be
> quite without drawbacks.

Yes it does. WRT those weapons; because, first, you are not guaranteed to
get them, then, you may not be proficient enough to get most out of them.
WRT the dragon scales; you must be proficient enough to kill the dragons
to get them, nothing that every character will be able to do equally well.
You typically don't get the powerful artifacts easily pre-Castle. Indeed,
dipping for Excalibur is, at least for lawfuls, quite safe - and I wonder
why that mechanics isn't restricted to Knights anyway - but still, there's
the fountain effects that you have to consider. But as I said; all factors
should be valued in the context of the game's complexity. And there are yet
more features and associated balances beyond weapons and armor; you can
lock your items in chests, but some monsters eat them, you can hide behind
locked doors or boulder, but there are monsters that dig and open paths,
you may collect a lot items and gold, but some monsters can steal them,
you may increase maxHP by nurse dancing, but you're vulnerable while doing
that, you may have powerful pets, but those may be separated from you (by
traps or level teleporters) and go feral, or they may eat a shape-changer
and become wimps. There are hundreds of factors in the game where, when
reflecting about them, are very well balanced. That is my observation and
valuation. I know that you have a different view (that I don't share, but
that's okay).

